}","line_end":236,"line_start":188,"recommendation":"If/when this code is ever made callable on-chain, gate all state-mutating functions (`set_pending_rewards`, `allocate`, and any funding/finalization hooks) behind a dedicated coordinator/admin authorization check (e.g., stored `Coordinator` address + `require_auth`). Consider keeping these functions non-public and only callable from validated internal entrypoints.","repo":"https://github.com/PeridotFinance/Peridot-Soroban","severity":"INFO","sha":"95c6e39bd1095d43cd4240b8ce7d8d1d82044aba","state":"OPEN","title":"Coordinator hooks callable without any authorization"},{"category":"","description":"Each distinct `owner` allocated units gets a persistent storage entry (`BackingKey::HybridBackingOwner(owner)`), and `set_owner` intentionally preserves even zero-value records. If `allocate` is ever callable by untrusted users (see missing auth finding), an attacker can create arbitrarily many persistent entries by allocating minimal units to many fresh addresses, growing contract state across transactions.

Exploit path (if exposed): attacker funds small amounts to create `unallocated_units`, then repeatedly calls `allocate` with new random `Address` values, forcing persistent writes + TTL extensions for each new key.

Practical impact is limited by Soroban fees/rent paid by the attacker and the fact the contract doesn’t iterate over all owners, but it can still bloat long-lived state.","file_path":"peridot-contracts/contracts/receipt-vault/src/reward_backing.rs","id":"bda14126-bd4f-40ab-9b77-9be7395024c4","impacted_code":"fn set_owner(env: &Env, owner: &Address, value: u128) {
